WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.971
A tool gives a program an ability it needs for a particular job.

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.953
A web search can find a recent notice.

00:00:07.953 --> 00:00:13.407
You still need to read the source and check whether it covers the right place and date.

00:00:13.407 --> 00:00:16.962
A calculator does arithmetic using rules.

00:00:16.962 --> 00:00:21.424
It is useful when a single wrong digit changes the answer.

00:00:21.424 --> 00:00:28.061
The surrounding program still has to select the right numbers and explain what the result measures.

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:32.851
A file reader opens a document the program is allowed to use.

00:00:32.851 --> 00:00:36.844
A shop policy may exist only in that document.

00:00:36.844 --> 00:00:43.265
Giving a helper a policy to read does not require giving it access to every file.

00:00:43.265 --> 00:00:49.569
Imagine a group asks Orbit about buying seven items priced at twenty-four dollars each.

00:00:49.569 --> 00:00:52.412
There is also news of a carrier delay.

00:00:52.412 --> 00:00:58.898
The customer wants the total, whether the delay affects delivery, and the shop’s return terms.

00:00:58.898 --> 00:01:05.084
Split that message into three questions because each needs a different kind of evidence.

00:01:05.084 --> 00:01:10.314
For the carrier question, search for a current notice and check the affected route.

00:01:10.314 --> 00:01:14.713
For the total, send twenty-four times seven to a calculator.

00:01:14.713 --> 00:01:21.053
Seven groups of twenty dollars make one hundred forty; seven groups of four add twenty-eight.

00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:27.462
The result is one hundred sixty-eight dollars before any unspecified charges.

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:32.446
The file reader lets Scout open Orbit's documents, and only those.

00:01:32.446 --> 00:01:36.758
Scout asks for a file by name, such as the policy file.

00:01:36.758 --> 00:01:43.500
Your code joins that name to the docs folder and resolves it into a full, absolute path.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:49.082
Then it checks that the result is still inside docs, and refuses if it is not.

00:01:49.082 --> 00:01:59.689
It rejects names that point outside the approved document folder. For this exercise a policy question needs access only to that folder.

00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:04.532
Only then does it read the file and send the text back to Scout.

00:02:04.532 --> 00:02:10.243
Imagine answering a customer who wants both a total and a shipping update.

00:02:10.243 --> 00:02:12.756
Write down the arithmetic first.

00:02:12.756 --> 00:02:16.739
Seven times twenty-four gives one hundred sixty-eight.

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:22.204
Yesterday's news or a policy document cannot improve that calculation.

00:02:22.204 --> 00:02:25.036
The second part needs a different source.

00:02:25.036 --> 00:02:32.964
A public delay notice might cover another region, so read the route and date before applying it to this order.

00:02:32.964 --> 00:02:37.308
A search result is the start of a check, not its conclusion.

00:02:37.308 --> 00:02:43.572
If the customer also asks about returning an item, open the shop's approved policy.

00:02:43.572 --> 00:02:47.182
Point each sentence of the reply back to its source.

00:02:47.182 --> 00:02:54.392
Splitting the question this way helps you give each tool only the access its own job requires.

00:02:54.392 --> 00:02:58.147
Put the three side by side and a pattern appears.

00:02:58.147 --> 00:03:04.061
Web search reaches the public web, so you cap it and switch it on only where it is needed.

00:03:04.061 --> 00:03:09.758
The calculator reaches numbers only, guarded by an allow-list of operators.

00:03:09.758 --> 00:03:14.166
The file reader reaches one folder, guarded by the path check.

00:03:14.166 --> 00:03:21.754
Compare a tool that runs any Python the model writes: it reaches everything, and there is no simple guard.

00:03:21.754 --> 00:03:28.412
The principle is called least privilege: give each tool the least power that still does the job.

00:03:28.412 --> 00:03:35.314
For the return terms, open the shop’s approved policy document and find the relevant passage.

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:42.900
A carrier notice cannot tell you the shop’s return window, and the calculator cannot promise a delivery date.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.665
Build the reply from the three results.

00:03:45.665 --> 00:03:50.328
If the notice does not mention this route, leave that part open.
